# Field Data to Dashboard: End-to-End Guide

Nov 5, 2026 · @Denish

This guide explains how field teams record locations and photos on a phone and see them appear on the VNV dashboard automatically. It uses only open-source software, running on our own cloud server.

## In one minute

- **What it does:** a field person stands on a spot and records it on a phone with photos and a short form. The record then appears on the VNV dashboard map. Nobody copies files by hand.
- **How:** the phone runs a free mapping app called **QField**. When the phone has mobile data, the app sends new records to our own **cloud server**, and the dashboard reads from that server.
- **Phase 1 (now): phone only.** The phone's own GPS is accurate to about 5 metres. That is enough for geotagged photos, site visits and field observations.
- **Phase 2 (later): add an RTK receiver** (a GPS antenna on a pole) with correction signals from Survey of India. This brings accuracy to about 3 centimetres for plot boundaries and areas. The app, server and dashboard stay exactly the same.
- **No signal in the field?** No problem. Records stay safe on the phone and send themselves once the phone is back online.
- **Cost:** all software is open source, so there are no licence fees. Phase 1 needs only the cloud server rent. Phase 2 adds the receiver hardware and the Survey of India service.
- **Office side, all in one place:** creating projects, designing forms, adding field persons, sending notices to their phones, reviewing, exporting and closing projects all happen in one VNV dashboard.

## The big picture

The office sets things up once. After that, field persons only record, and sending to the dashboard happens on its own.

