**Phase 1: decisions needed**

- [ ] Approve a cloud server (provider, account) and a web address for it
- [ ] Decide who in the office is admin, and who is reviewer

**Phase 1: setup work**

- [ ] Install QFieldCloud, PostGIS, MinIO and ntfy on the cloud server, with HTTPS and daily backups
- [ ] Build the VNV field dashboard: projects, form templates, people, notices, review, export, progress, close
- [ ] Create the VNV layer templates (Farm plots, Trees, Soil samples, Water points)
- [ ] Create the first project in the dashboard, with plot boundaries marked "approximate"
- [ ] Office test: 3 points with photos appear on the dashboard
- [ ] Notice test: a form update goes through the full notice flow on two phones
- [ ] Short training for field persons using Step 2 of this guide

**Later versions**

- [ ] PDF report with photos, from the dashboard
- [ ] Phase 2 RTK: choose and buy receiver(s), register for Survey of India CORS, confirm site coverage, run an accuracy test on a known survey mark, and re-survey phase 1 plot boundaries that will be used in carbon claims

## Why QField

QField was chosen because it is open source, works offline, connects to RTK receivers without extra apps, and syncs to a server we control.

**Compared with SW Maps** (the app first suggested): SW Maps is free but closed source, and it can only send data by manual upload to a file (FTP) server. QField matches it on almost everything field teams use: points, lines and areas, forms with photos, offline maps, measuring, averaging and stakeout. It adds automatic syncing, an accuracy gate, stricter forms, and bearing-and-distance tools. Two things SW Maps has that QField does not:

- **Raw GPS data logging for later processing.** Matters only in phase 2: if a site has no signal for corrections, use a receiver that stores raw data internally.
- **Creating new forms on the phone in the field.** With QField, forms are prepared in the office, which keeps data consistent across the team.

**Other open-source options considered:**

| App + server | Good at | Why not first choice |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Mergin Maps](https://merginmaps.com/docs/misc/licensing/) | Simple QGIS-based field app with sync | Fewer survey features than QField |
| [ODK Collect + ODK Central](https://github.com/getodk/central) | Very proven questionnaire surveys | Weak for drawing plots on a map; RTK only through a separate helper app |
| [KoboToolbox](https://support.kobotoolbox.org/api) | Forms; already familiar at VNV | Same limits as ODK |

